  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over the period 2022-23, the Trade Union Council of Namibia (TUCNA), National Union of Namibian Workers (NUNW) and the Namibia National Labour Organisation (NANLO) developed and presented joint proposals on social protection and pensions reforms in Namibia: 
1.In 2022, the Federations developed a joint position on the proposed introduction of compulsory preservation of withdrawal benefits for workers who withdraw from their retirement funds before retirement under the Financial Institutions and Market Act (FIMA).
a. A position paper was submitted to Namibia Financial Institutions Supervisory Authority (NAMFISA) in April 2022 following a call to provide input on the draft standards and regulations.
b.In May 2023, because of the representations by workers, the Government appointed a technical advisory committee (TAC) that includes federations of trade unions and employers to consult on the proposed regulations. The paper recommended the building financial literacy among workers and the public on utilization and investments of pensions and retirement funds.
2.In 2022, the Trade Union Federations developed a joint position on the broad social protection reforms in Namibia.
a.The paper was developed as input to the on-going dialogues on extending coverage of social protection to the majority workers in the informal and rural economies and to highlight the need to strengthen the effective representation of workers in the administration and governance of national social security bodies.
b.Despite some representation of workers in for example the Government institutions Pension Fund (GIPF) board, the Basic Income Grant Coalition (BIG) and the Social Security Commission (SSC) Board recent policy reforms have not been in the interest of workers.
c.The position paper recommends an inclusive approach to social protection and a code of conduct for workers&#x2019; representatives. In 2023, this approach has been adopted in the on-going dialogue on the National Pension Fund (NPF). 1. The ILO through funding from ILO Regular Budget Supplementary Account (RBSA) and Regular Budgetary Technical Cooperation (RBTC) resources recruited consultants to draft the position papers which were validated by the trade union federations in two (2) workshops in November 2022</narrative>
   </description>
